## 2 How To Read This Guide (What Actually Matters)

"FDA-cleared" on the box is not the useful filter. Three questions are:

1. **Can the exact model be tied to a 510(k) K-number?** Several heavily marketed devices, including Wirecutter's and Fortune's top mask picks, could not be.
2. **Has its output been measured independently?** Measured output ran from close to the claim (Omnilux) to 38% under it (CurrentBody Series 2) to essentially zero (FOREO FAQ 202).
3. **Did trials test this wavelength, format and schedule?** For the face that means 630–660 nm red, with or without 830–850 nm near-infrared. For hair it means 650–655 nm laser diodes in a comb, cap or helmet on the scalp.

Then match the refund window to the biology. Mask results separate from sham around week 8, so 60–120 days is enough to judge a mask. Hair trials measure at 16–26 weeks, so any refund window shorter than six months closes before you can tell whether a hair device works.

## 3 What the Evidence Supports

Study details below come from PubMed-indexed papers and FDA 510(k) summaries; DOIs and FDA links are in Section 12.

### 3.1 Hair (androgenetic alopecia)

**Meta-analyses agree the effect is real and modest.**

- **Alahmadi 2026** (37 studies, 3,298 patients): +18.56 hairs/cm² and +10.62 µm of shaft thickness over sham, with mild, transient side effects.
- **Perez 2024** (38 studies, 3,098 patients): standardized effect 1.14 before 20 weeks and 1.44 after, with very high heterogeneity (I² 81–88%).
- **Cochrane, van Zuuren 2016** (women): laser combs added 17.4 and 17.6 hairs/cm² over sham in two trials, against 13.18 for minoxidil over placebo. Quality was rated low, and participants did not rate their own improvement as significantly better.
- **Lueangarun 2021** (FDA-cleared home devices only): laser-only devices (SMD 1.52) outperformed laser-plus-LED hybrids (0.85, p=0.043). Gupta & Carviel 2019 found the same direction and no difference between combs and helmets.
- **Network meta-analyses** rate the certainty for light versus sham "very low" (Gupta 2018). The newest one in men ranks oral finasteride and dutasteride above light therapy (Gupta 2024).
- **The European S3 guideline** suggests light therapy only "as ancillary therapy" and makes no recommendation beyond six months.

**Every device line with a sham-controlled trial was tested by its own maker.**

| Device lineage | Trial | Design | Result vs sham | Funding |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| HairMax LaserComb, 655 nm | Leavitt 2009 | 26 weeks, men, 110 completers | Greater terminal-hair density, p<0.0001 | Maker |
| HairMax 7-, 9-, 12-beam combs | Jimenez 2014 | Four trials, 269 men and women, 26 weeks | Net +11.5 to +17.6 hairs/cm² | Maker |
| iGrow prototype (21 lasers + 30 LEDs, 655 nm) | Lanzafame 2013, 2014 | 44 men; 47 women; 16 weeks | +39% and +37% relative hair count | Device maker |
| Capillus272 (272 lasers, 650 nm) | Friedman 2017 | Women only, 40 analyzed, 30 min every other day, 17 weeks | +51% relative hair count | Maker |
| REVIAN (620/660 nm LEDs) | Thomas 2024 | 160 randomized, 69 analyzed, 26 weeks | No single arm significant; arms pooled +28.5 hairs/cm² | Maker-affiliated authors |

These trials were short, counted hairs in a small tattooed patch, and mostly used glowing incandescent shams.

**Most cleared caps have no trial at all.** FDA has cleared 112 hair-laser devices since 2007. iRestore's first clearance states "No clinical performance data was produced" and relies on the iGrow trials. Kiierr's clearance also had no clinical data and describes the cap as identical to a factory private-label device.

**Other findings that decide purchases**

- **Laser versus LED:** no trial compares them at matched wavelength and dose. The laser advantage comes only from cross-trial subgroups. The trial evidence sits mostly with lasers, so an evidence-driven buyer should choose lasers.
- **Diode count:** a regression across trials found diode count "not significantly associated" with density gains (Gupta 2020). Each home diode is capped near 5 mW, so "total mW" mostly restates the diode count.
- **With minoxidil:** conflicting. One 2025 meta-analysis found +6.62 hairs/cm² from adding a cap (Mawu 2025); another found no added benefit (Alosaimi 2025). Alone, a 633 nm device was statistically indistinguishable from 5% minoxidil at six months in a 91-man trial (Neema 2024).
- **After stopping:** no controlled follow-up exists. A Stanford dermatologist says the effects stop when the light stops.
- **Panels:** no trial of any free-standing panel for pattern hair loss was found.
- **Who it can help:** every hair clearance covers only men at Norwood-Hamilton IIa–V, women at Ludwig-Savin I–II, and Fitzpatrick skin types I–IV. Long-bald or scarred scalp will not respond.

### 3.2 Face (fine lines and wrinkles)

**Systematic reviews give wrinkles a middling grade.**

- **Jagdeo 2018** (31 randomized LED trials) graded skin rejuvenation **C**, below acne at B. Two of the six rejuvenation trials found no difference from control. Two authors sat on the advisory board of Omnilux's maker.
- **Ngoc 2022** pooled a large effect for acne and reported no pooled wrinkle estimate.
- **Cohen 2021**, a review of home devices, recommended LEDs for acne but not for wrinkles.
- **A 2025 JAAD expert consensus** lists hair loss among light therapy's effective uses; facial aging is not on that list in its abstract.

**The sham-controlled trials cut both ways.**

- **Park 2025**, the strongest home-mask trial: 60 people, double-blind, multicenter; 630 + 850 nm at no more than 10 mW/cm², 9 minutes five times a week for 12 weeks. Crow's feet improved by at least one grade in 86.2% of users versus 16.7% on sham. **Funded by Y&J Bio, the mask's maker.** A gap that large is implausible for this field and argues for caution.
- **Bragato 2025**, the largest independent trial: 95 women, 660 nm mask at 6.4 mW/cm² versus sham for four weeks. **No difference on the blinded wrinkle scale**; only software-measured line length and satisfaction improved.
- **Lee 2007**: 76 people, double-blind, split-face. Wrinkles fell 26% with 633 nm, 33% with 830 nm and 36% with both, from baseline. The device brand and funding could not be verified.
- **Wunsch 2014**: 136 people, non-contact lamps at 6–13 mW/cm² red. Blinded raters saw improvement in 69–75% of treated versus 4% of untreated controls. No sham, and fully funded by the lamp maker's parent company.
- **Negative results:** a 588 nm amber device was judged "a placebo" (Boulos 2009); an Omnilux Revive split-face trial found no objective change (Bhat 2005); 660 nm red was no better than white light (Nam 2017).

**What to take from this**

- Choose 630–660 nm red, with or without 830–850 nm near-infrared. That is where the controlled data sit. The 1072 nm diodes in Omnilux Men and CurrentBody rest on one self-reported 2007 trial and a seven-person pilot. Green and multi-color modes have no wrinkle trials.
- Do not pay extra for irradiance. The positive sham-controlled home trials ran at 6–10 mW/cm², and positive studies span 0.1 to 126 J/cm² per session with no dose-response curve.
- Expect slow, modest results that depend on continued use. Park's mask separated from sham at week 8. Prescription retinoids remain the best-studied treatment for photoaging, and no trial has compared a mask with tretinoin.
- Red and near-infrared have weak evidence for acne and none for melasma. Blue 415 nm has acne evidence but can darken skin (Section 9).

### 3.3 What FDA 510(k) clearance means

- **Substantial equivalence, not approval.** Clearance means FDA found the device as safe and effective as an older predicate device. Federal rules state it "does not in any way denote official approval."
- **Often no clinical data.** Shark CryoGlow's and CurrentBody Series 2's clearances rested on bench, electrical and biocompatibility testing. Most hair-cap clearances submitted no clinical data.
- **Shared factories.** One Shenzhen manufacturer, Kaiyan, holds the clearances behind the HigherDOSE mask and hat, CurrentBody Series 2 and CurrentBody's dual-light helmet. A brand premium often pays for design and service rather than different light.
- **Product codes.** Hair devices clear under OAP; wrinkle masks under OHS; acne masks under OLP. "FDA Class II registered" on a wellness panel usually means the 510(k)-exempt infrared-lamp code (ILY), which covers heat and pain, not wrinkles or hair.

## 4 Face: The Picks

### 4.1 Best face mask: Omnilux Contour Face ($395)

- **Light:** 633 nm red plus 830 nm near-infrared from 66 dual-chip bulbs, marketed as "132 LEDs". Claims about 30 mW/cm². One calibrated-spectrometer reading, reported second-hand by LightTherapyIQ, is about 28 mW/cm², among the smallest claim-to-reality gaps in the category.
- **Use:** 10 minutes, 3–5 times a week, in a flexible silicone shell with a battery controller on a cable.
- **FDA:** FDA's device-identifier database links the retail model to **K191629**, cleared in September 2019 for "the treatment of full-face wrinkles."
- **Testers:** Yahoo Shopping's 10-month, six-mask test named it best overall in September 2026. Fortune calls it best for wrinkles. Wirecutter's flexible-mask pick is its sibling, the Omnilux Men.
- **Weak point:** a 2-year warranty, but **returns only within 30 days and only if unopened.** Yahoo's tester also reported poor customer service.

**Get it if** you want the mask with the most consistent tester support, verified output and a traceable clearance, and you accept that you cannot return it once opened.

### 4.2 Shark CryoGlow ($349.99): acne mode and a real trial period

Cleared (**K242796**) for wrinkles with red and infrared light, and for mild-to-moderate inflammatory acne with red, blue and infrared light. It has a 2-year warranty, a 60-day money-back guarantee and chilled under-eye pads. Programs run 6 minutes for aging and 8 for acne. Shark publishes no wavelengths or irradiance, and its clinical support is a 12-week, 119-person study with no control group. Testers split: Wirecutter's rigid-mask pick and Men's Health's best overall, but Yahoo's tester found the hard shell uncomfortable. It also accounts for 69 of the 107 FDA adverse-event reports under the wrinkle-mask code, mostly irritation, discoloration, burning or eye irritation; high sales and batch filing of foreign reports likely inflate that count.

**Get it if** you want acne treatment in the same mask and a 60-day window to decide.

### 4.3 HigherDOSE Red Light Face Mask ($349): longest refund, published output

Publishes irradiance per wavelength: 26 mW/cm² red plus 24 mW/cm² near-infrared, a 50 mW/cm² total that GembaRed's test matched (GembaRed sells panels, so it is not a neutral lab). Red and near-infrared only, cordless, cleared for full-face wrinkles (**K241933**), with a **120-day money-back guarantee**, the longest among the flagships.

**Get it if** you want to try a mask with the least risk of being stuck with it.

### 4.4 Solawave Wrinkle Retreat Pro ($399): 3-minute sessions

605, 630, 660 and 830 nm from 320 LEDs; 3-minute sessions; cleared for full-face wrinkles (**K252983**, December 2025); 1-year warranty; 60-day refund with free return shipping. Its claimed 65 mW/cm² has not been independently measured.

**Get it if** three minutes is the difference between using a mask and not.

### 4.5 Others, and what to avoid

- **Omnilux Clear ($395):** 415/633 nm, cleared for acne (K210948). The only branded mask with a peer-reviewed study, an open-label 30-patient acne trial. Buy it if acne, not wrinkles, is the goal.
- **Dr. Dennis Gross DRx SpectraLite FaceWare Pro ($455):** 3-minute sessions, 1-year warranty, 30-day refund. Its clearance is probably K183247 but could not be confirmed.
